            <ArticleTitle>Relapse of prostate cancer from the viewpoint of total gland volume kinetics theory.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>904-7; discussion 907</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/1008-682X.153543</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In many cases of prostate cancer, changes of prostate volume were measured very frequently after castration by means of transrectal ultrasound. For short-term observations, a factor called as &quot;reduction time (tau [t]),&quot; which means the time required for the volume of &quot;effective portion&quot; (volume responded to castration) to be reduced to one-tenth of its original value, correlated very well with patients' prognosis. For long-term observations for relapse, changes of prostate volume could be divided clearly into two types, namely, &quot;metastatic progressive type&quot; with metastasis without volume increase and &quot;local progressive type&quot; with remarkable volume increase without metastasis. &quot;t&quot; in the &quot;local progressive type&quot; was far shorter than that in the &quot;metastatic progressive type.&quot; Much longer survival was obtained when the patient belonged to the &quot;local progressive type,&quot; than to the &quot;metastatic progressive type.&quot; Thus, &quot;t&quot; was the most important and the most reliable factor to predict the prognosis. Based upon these observations, a schematic analysis of prostate cancer relapse was shown.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
